    {
      "title": "Ordinary dividends and deposited-dividend interest",
      "text": "A dividend is generally a premium return until the relevant investment is recovered. Interest credited on deposited ordinary insurance dividends is generally taxable separately. Veterans’ insurance interest has special federal treatment; do not generalize that exception to commercial life policies.",
      "example": "The example’s $800 eligible premium return and $40 interest have different tax classifications.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.02"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Loans, withdrawals and lapse",
      "text": "Under ordinary non-MEC rules, an in-force policy loan is generally debt. A withdrawal ordinarily recovers investment first, but benefit-reduction rules during the first 15 years can alter that result. Lapse or surrender can treat an outstanding loan and interest discharge as a distribution, creating taxable gain without equivalent new cash.",
      "example": "A loan described as nontaxable when made is not a promise that every later lapse is nontaxable.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.01",
        "7.1.03",
        "7.1.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Full surrender is not a death claim",
      "text": "Ordinary surrender income is the amount treated as received above the applicable investment in the contract. Income is ordinary, not converted into capital gain by a long holding period. The carrier generally reports the transaction on Form 1099-R. A personal policy loss does not automatically establish a deductible investment loss.",
      "example": "With $55,000 proceeds, $40,000 adjusted basis and no debt or other effects, taxable gain is $15,000.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.04"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Death-benefit exclusion has exceptions",
      "text": "Section 101 generally excludes amounts paid because of the insured’s death. The transfer-for-value rule can limit the exclusion to consideration paid plus later premiums and specified amounts. There are statutory exceptions, including certain carryover-basis transfers and transfers to the insured, a partner, an eligible partnership or corporation; reportable policy sales can override those exceptions. Employer-owned life insurance has separate notice, consent and eligibility requirements.",
      "example": "A sale to an unrelated investor is not interchangeable with an ordinary family beneficiary designation.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.05"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Calculate fixed installments separately from interest-only or life income",
      "text": "For a stated fixed number of death-benefit installments, divide the amount held at death by that number to determine the excluded portion under the applicable rule. Amounts above it are interest. A life-contingent option requires the appropriate life-expectancy and guarantee adjustment calculations. Buying a separate annuity after receiving a lump sum is a different tax transaction.",
      "example": "A $200,000 benefit divided across 100 stated installments gives $2,000 excluded per installment; a $2,400 payment therefore includes $400 interest.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.06"
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Gross estate is a different tax concept",
      "text": "Insurance payable to or for the estate and insurance subject to retained incidents of ownership can be included in the insured’s federal gross estate. Certain transfers within three years of death also matter. Inclusion is not itself a calculation of tax payable. Federal and Maryland estate rules are separate, and annual dollar thresholds must be dated when used.",
      "example": "Naming an individual beneficiary does not eliminate the need to examine the insured’s retained ownership powers.",
      "topics": [
        "7.1.07"
      ]
    }
